What:           /sys/class/accel/accel*/device/performance_level
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        asahi@lists.linux.dev
Description:
                Requested ANE performance level, 0 through 5, or "unsupported"
                when the device tree does not describe a supported controller.
                This is a firmware request, not a measured clock frequency.
                Work raises the level from the SoC match data. It drops to 0
                after 32 ms without a submission. A stream amortizes the clock
                transition cost; the first job after idle includes that cost.
                The runtime PM reference covers the entire request lifetime.

What:           /sys/class/accel/accel*/device/memory_vote
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        asahi@lists.linux.dev
Description:
                Requested average and peak memory bandwidth, in decimal kB/s,
                separated by a space. Both are zero after idle release. The
                requests are aggregated with other interconnect consumers.
                The active values come from the high-level OPP's opp-avg-kBps
                and opp-peak-kBps. "unsupported" means no interconnect path is
                described. It does not mean a successful zero-bandwidth vote.
                Current T600x trees have no host DCS interconnect provider;
                describing a path requires a verified shared voting transport.

What:           /sys/class/accel/accel*/device/performance_error
Date:           October 2026
Contact:        asahi@lists.linux.dev
Description:
                Last performance transition result: zero on success or a
                negative errno. Failed busy requests abort before enqueueing.
                Failed idle releases retain the runtime PM reference, retry
                up to three times, and remain visible here if still failing.
                A subsequent workload retries release before requesting high.
                An unknown DMA state retains the votes until recovery.
